    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they pretend the future is more predictable than it is. They often present a neat sequence of features, dates, and milestones, creating the impression that product development is a matter of executing a known plan. In reality, products are built in uncertain environments. Customer needs change, competitors move, technical constraints emerge, and ideas that sound convincing in a meeting may fail when tested.\n\nThe first problem is that many roadmaps focus on outputs rather than outcomes. They promise features instead of describing the customer or business problem those features are meant to solve. This encourages teams to measure progress by how much they ship, not by whether the product becomes more useful. A feature can be delivered on time and still have no meaningful impact.\n\nRoadmaps also become political documents. Sales teams want commitments they can share with prospects. Executives want evidence that strategic goals are being addressed. Customers want reassurance that their requests are coming. Product leaders often respond by adding more items and assigning dates before enough is known. The roadmap becomes a collection of promises to different stakeholders rather than a coherent expression of product strategy.\n\nAnother reason roadmaps fail is that they separate planning from learning. Teams may spend months building an item because it appears on the roadmap, even after research suggests that the underlying assumption is wrong. Changing direction can look like poor execution, so people continue with the plan to avoid difficult conversations. The roadmap, which should support decision-making, instead prevents it.\n\nOverloaded roadmaps create a further problem. When everything is a priority, nothing is. Teams divide their attention across too many initiatives, dependencies multiply, and delivery slows. Dates begin to slip, confidence falls, and managers respond by demanding more detailed estimates. This adds administrative effort without reducing uncertainty.\n\nA useful roadmap should not claim to know exactly what will be built far into the future. It should explain which problems matter, why they matter, what outcomes the organization seeks, and what assumptions need to be tested. Near-term work can be specific because the team has more evidence. Longer-term plans should remain flexible.\n\nMost roadmaps do not fail because teams lack discipline. They fail because certainty is rewarded more than honesty. A roadmap succeeds when it helps people make better choices as new information arrives, not when it preserves an outdated plan.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail for a simple reason: they are asked to do two jobs that pull in opposite directions. One job is to help a team decide what to build next. The other is to reassure people who are not on the team that the future is under control. The second job almost always wins, and once it does, the roadmap stops being a tool and becomes a performance.\n\nYou can see this in how roadmaps get made. Someone senior wants to know what is shipping in Q3. A product manager, wanting to look competent, fills in the boxes. Sales wants a date for the feature a big customer asked about, so a date appears. Engineering is asked to estimate work that has not been scoped, and they guess, knowing the guess will be treated as a commitment. By the time the document is finished, it is a list of promises made to people who each heard what they wanted to hear. Nobody lied, exactly. But nobody was really planning either.\n\nThe deeper problem is that a roadmap encodes a level of certainty that does not exist. Six months out, you do not know what customers will ask for, what a competitor will launch, which assumption in your current bet will turn out to be wrong, or which two engineers will leave. Everyone knows this. Yet the format of a roadmap, with its neat quarters and confident labels, quietly erases that knowledge. It looks like a schedule, so it gets treated like one. When reality diverges, which it always does, the roadmap is either abandoned or defended. Both outcomes are bad. Abandoned, it teaches the organization that planning is theater. Defended, it forces teams to ship things they already know are wrong because changing course would look like failure.\n\nRoadmaps also tend to list features instead of problems. A feature is easy to put on a timeline and easy to check off. A problem is messier, because you might discover halfway through that the feature you picked does not actually solve it. But a feature list gives a team no way to think. When the plan says \"build export to CSV\" and a user interview reveals that nobody actually wants CSV, they want to stop needing the export at all, the roadmap has no room for that finding. It was written to be executed, not to be learned from.\n\nAnd then there is capacity. Almost every roadmap I have seen assumes the team spends all of its time on roadmap items. It ignores bugs, support escalations, the migration that cannot wait, the thing the CEO saw at a conference. Unplanned work is not an exception; it is a fixed cost, usually a third of the team's time or more. A plan that pretends otherwise is late from the day it is published.\n\nNone of this means roadmaps are useless. A good one states what the team is trying to learn and what it currently believes, and it invites disagreement. It gets shorter and vaguer the further out it goes, because that is honest. The roadmaps that fail are the ones that were never allowed to be honest in the first place.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
